D. Pelvic ultrasonography.

Pelvic ultrasonography is the best initial investigation for evaluating an irregular uterus. The ultrasound can provide valuable information about the anatomy of the uterus and the endometrial thickness. In a 28-year-old woman, irregularity of the uterus could be due to a variety of causes, including fibroids, polyps, or adenomyosis. Although the patient does not have symptoms of heavy bleeding or severe pain, she should still be evaluated using non-invasive imaging as a first step because asymptomatic fibroids, polyps, or luteal cysts can cause an irregularly shaped uterus.

Of the other options given:
A. Continued observation is not appropriate in the absence of any significant findings on examination and Pap smear and especially since the woman has an irregular uterus,
B. An endometrial biopsy is considered only after there is suspicion of malignancy or when the patient has risk factors for endometrial cancer,
C. Hysterectomy is a radical measure and doesn't fit into the appropriate management in the early stage of presentation,
E. Laparoscopy is an invasive surgical procedure and is typically considered after establishing a clear diagnosis and excluding other alternatives.

