outputs:
 A.  a.	Antibody type is predominantly IgG.

Rationale: Warm autoimmune haemolytic anemia (WAIHA) is characterized by the presence of cold-reactive antibodies directed against RBC surface antigens. However, in the setting of warm temperature (above body temperature), these antibodies might bind more strongly and cause RBC lysis. The preferred antibodies involved in WAIHA is IgG which binds to RBCs at normal body temperatures and subsequently leads to their destruction through hemolysis. The other options are incorrect. B refers to cold autoimmune haemolytic anemia (CAIHA) which often presents in infants. C may be true, but it is not the most characteristic feature. Cold weather can exacerbate CAIHA due to More reason, whereas WAIHA remains active in normal temperatures. D can be part of management advice for CAIHA patients, not WAIHA. E is incorrect as corticosteroid therapy is a common treatment for WAIHA to decrease the immune response.

